-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## Eviction Policy
+
+The cache uses 2Q to separate first-use sheets from sheets that have proven reusable. The reasoning, alternatives, and criteria behind that choice are recorded in `decisions/eviction-policy.md`.
+
+The cache is organized as two queues and one key-only list:
+
+- The **first-use queue** holds sheets that have not yet been requested a second time, ordered by admission.
+- The **main queue** holds sheets that have been requested at least twice, ordered by last access.
+- The **ghost list** holds keys of sheets that were evicted to make room. It stores keys only, never JPEG data.
+
+Admission and promotion:
+
+- A miss for a key that is in neither queue nor the ghost list renders the sheet and admits it to the first-use queue.
+- A hit for a key in the first-use queue promotes the sheet into the main queue, carrying its bytes with it.
+- A miss for a key in the ghost list renders the sheet and admits it directly to the main queue, because a request after eviction is proof of reuse that the first-use queue would otherwise discard. The key leaves the ghost list at that point.
+- The ghost list is bounded at 10,000 keys; when it is full, the oldest key is dropped.
+
+Eviction:
+
+- The byte budget is split between the queues: the first-use queue is capped at 3 GiB and the main queue at 9 GiB, together the 12 GiB cache budget. The cap applies to cached JPEG payload; queue metadata is not counted against it.
+- When admitting to the first-use queue and it is at its cap, entries are evicted from the tail of that queue — the earliest admissions first — until the new sheet fits.
+- When admitting to the main queue, by promotion or by ghost-list admission, and it is at its cap, the least recently used main-queue entry is evicted.
+- The key of every evicted sheet is recorded in the ghost list, whichever queue it was evicted from. A later request for that sheet is then admitted directly to the main queue rather than starting over as first-use.
+- Admission and eviction are serialized within the worker, so the caps hold when several renders complete at once.
+
+Replacement metadata — queue membership, ordering, and ghost keys — stays in the worker. No other node reads it, and no other node can request an entry's retention.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with a key-only ghost list | Least recently used; frequency-based eviction with aging | A sequential screening and a comparison session look identical to LRU, so the budget follows whichever touched it last and the comparison sheets are the entries a scan displaces. Aged frequency counters retain popular sheets through history but require per-clip counter maintenance and aging, and they lag a project switch. 2Q protects reused sheets structurally by segregating them, at the cost of extra per-entry state and a tunable size split. See `decisions/eviction-policy.md`. |
+| Reuse detection after eviction | Ghost list of evicted first-use keys | Two queues with no history | Without the ghost list, a sheet evicted from the first-use queue and requested again would start over as first-use and could be evicted by the next scan, which is the failure the policy exists to prevent. |
+| Queue size split | 3 GiB first-use, 9 GiB main, 10,000 ghost keys | Even split; single queue with a reserved region | The first-use queue is the part of the cache a screening scan fills, so it is the part that should yield first. The split is a configurable parameter whose starting values are defaults to be tuned against observed workloads, not fixed requirements. |
